  body: [
    ["What it is", "Teaching material written the same day the CS237A workstation was built, for a reader who knows nothing about ROS. Fifteen sections: why robot software is split into processes at all, the five core abstractions (nodes, topics, services, actions, parameters), the plumbing that actually breaks (discovery, QoS, names, simulation time), coordinate frames and tf2, URDF against SDF, the colcon overlay model, launch files, Gazebo and its bridge, pose-graph SLAM, then a layer-by-layer account of the install, a line-by-line annotation of the real log, a command reference and a glossary."],
    ["The pedagogical bet", "Every concept is attached to a line of output Anshu personally watched scroll past, rather than to a generic tutorial example. A ROS tutorial is easy to find and hard to retain because the concepts stay abstract; the fastest way to make them stick is to explain the warning that was already on screen. Section 12 annotates the entire run end to end, including which warnings are structural and permanently ignorable."],
    ["The one constraint that shapes everything", "ROS 2 Humble ships as Ubuntu 22.04 binaries, so the whole installation is downstream of that single fact: Ubuntu 22.04 specifically, FROM ros:humble, and the much longer Mac path in the course doc, which needs a full VM because a Mac cannot execute Ubuntu binaries at all. State the constraint and the architecture stops looking arbitrary."],
    ["What the install actually built", "Five layers, each solving a problem created by the one below: Windows 11, WSL 2 (real Linux kernel, WSLg for GUI windows, /dev/dxg for the GPU), the Docker daemon, the dev container (ROS Humble + Gazebo Garden + 20 baked dependencies), and the bind-mounted workspace holding 18 built packages. The bind mount is the load-bearing detail: the compiled workspace lives on WSL disk, not inside the container, so a container rebuild cannot destroy it."],
    ["The one real bug, and the general lesson", "rosdep was first run inside a container started with --rm. It reported complete success and the compiled workspace survived, because that was written to the bind mount — but all twenty apt packages were destroyed on exit, surfacing later as “package 'slam_toolbox' not found”. Fix: bake the resolved set into the Dockerfile. The general principle is stated as the takeaway — in container work, always know whether a change lives in the image or in the container — and it makes this setup strictly more durable than the course doc, under which any future rebuild silently loses slam_toolbox."],
    ["Numbers that came from this machine, not from a book", "The GPU chain is documented because it is non-obvious: WSL exposes the GPU at /dev/dxg rather than /dev/dri (so the doc's commented-out /dev/dri line could never have worked), and Mesa's d3d12 backend defaults to the integrated AMD GPU unless MESA_D3D12_DEFAULT_ADAPTER_NAME=NVIDIA is set. Verified renderer: D3D12 (NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 Ti Laptop GPU), OpenGL 4.2 against Gazebo's 3.3 requirement. The measured gain is deliberately undersold: physics is CPU-bound, so hardware GL mainly smooths the viewport."],
    ["What it does not do", "No new research, no web retrieval. The ROS concepts are standard published knowledge written from working understanding rather than re-read from docs.ros.org on the day, so treat version-level details as point-in-time. It is an orientation document, not a reference implementation, and section 15 says plainly that it confers no familiarity — only a second terminal and a running simulator do that."]
  ],
  files: [
    { p: "reports/ros2-primer-2026-09-27.md", d: "The primer, 8,305 words, fifteen sections plus a glossary and a command reference" }
  ]
