  body: [
    ["What it is", "Teaching material written the day after CS237A Section 1, for a reader with no prior Unix, git or GitHub knowledge. Nine sections in the house report format: what the section was really teaching, Linux against Windows and macOS, home versus working directories and absolute versus relative paths, the Unix commands grouped by purpose rather than alphabetically, the git mental model, why GitHub is not git, the loop to repeat all quarter, every error diagnosed, and a glossary."],
    ["The central diagram, and why it is the whole document", "Git keeps code in three local places plus one remote, and every command is a move between two adjacent ones: add copies working directory into staging, commit writes staging into the local repository, push sends commits to the remote, fetch downloads remote state without touching files, pull is fetch plus merge. The staging area exists so a commit can be one coherent idea rather than whatever happened to be on disk. The argument made explicitly is that holding this diagram lets you reason about reset, stash, rebase and cherry-pick without having met them, whereas holding a command list makes every new command a new thing to memorise."],
    ["Home directory versus working directory, which was the stated confusion", "A home directory is a fixed property of the user account, recorded in the system account database and exposed as $HOME and as the tilde. A working directory is a property of the running shell, changed by cd, and the origin of every relative path. They coincide at login, which is the entire reason they get conflated, and on this machine they do not even coincide there: VS Code opens container terminals at /home, one level ABOVE the home directory. Two home directories are both spelled /home/anshuarora and point at different places, because the container's /home is a bind mount of WSL's /home/anshuarora."],
    ["Linux against Windows and macOS, stated precisely", "The kernel-versus-userland split is the key: Linux is strictly only the kernel, which is why macOS can feel similar as a BSD Unix while differing in detail, and why its BSD coreutils accept different flags from Linux's GNU ones. Unix has one filesystem tree under / with devices at mount points rather than Windows' separate lettered trees, and that single design choice is what lets the container's /home BE WSL's home rather than merely resemble it. Linux filesystems are case-sensitive, so ReadME.md and README.md are different files, a distinction NTFS and default APFS volumes hide. Executability is a permission bit plus a shebang rather than a file extension."],
    ["Why the error list is the most useful section", "Nine real errors from one session, each with its one-line cause: command not found from PATH excluding the current directory, a zero-byte script from an unsaved editor buffer, outside repository from a leading slash making a path absolute, git add -u silently skipping untracked files, an unset identity cascading into three downstream failures, git init turning a 24 GB home directory into a repository, gh present on Windows but absent in the Linux container, and keystrokes vanishing into a foreground process's stdin. Not one was a broken tool. Every message was accurate and most commands did exactly as asked, which is the actual lesson."],
    ["What it does not do", "No new research and no robotics content; this is the infrastructure layer beneath the ROS primer rather than a continuation of it. Git and Unix facts are standard published knowledge with primary documentation cited inline, written from working understanding rather than re-read on the day. It is an orientation document and says plainly that it confers no fluency, which only repetition at a terminal provides."]
  ],
  files: [
    { p: "reports/linux-git-github-primer-2026-09-30.md", d: "The primer, 8,943 words, nine sections plus a glossary and the three-part closing" }
  ]
